WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.830
YURI GRINSHTEYN: We've seen how
to collect observability data

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.792
from services running
in Google Cloud.

00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:05.250
But what if you
still have services

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.155
on prem or in other clouds?

00:00:07.155 --> 00:00:09.030
Today, I'm going to show
you how to determine

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.970
the right observability strategy
for a hybrid or multi-cloud

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:12.850
environment.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:14.130
This is the "Stack Doctor."

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.518
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.390
Before we start, let's
talk about what hybrid

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.183
and multi-cloud really means.

00:00:25.183 --> 00:00:27.100
People often think that
hybrid and multi-cloud

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.750
means having a
single workload that

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.180
runs across multiple
infrastructure providers.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.760
But in practice, this
rarely works as advertised.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.070
The added latency and
cost of network egress

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:38.377
makes this largely impractical.

00:00:38.377 --> 00:00:40.210
We see much more success
with a hybrid model

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.820
where different providers are
used for different workloads

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:44.900
and, often, by different teams.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.630
However, this introduces
observability challenges.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.450
How can you standardize
things like SLOs,

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:53.080
observability, and alerting
across all these platforms?

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.070
Generally speaking,
you have two options.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:58.180
The first is to use Google Cloud
as the single pane of glass

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.330
and send your observability
data from other platforms there.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:03.730
There are well-documented
integration options for this,

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.400
and I'll get into those shortly.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.720
Your second option is to
use a third-party service

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.400
as your single pane
of glass and have

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.200
it consume observability
data that's either

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.300
collected by cloud
monitoring and logging

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.117
or emitted directly by your
Google Cloud resources.

00:01:17.117 --> 00:01:19.200
Let's review a few examples
for the first option--

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.720
using Google Cloud as
your single pane of glass.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.430
You may need to ingest
observability data

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:26.340
from multiple platforms,
monitor Kubernetes clusters,

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:29.050
or collect logging data
from multiple sources.

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.430
You can use BindPlane, a
solution from Blue Medora,

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:34.110
a Google Cloud Partner, to
import monitoring and logging

00:01:34.110 --> 00:01:37.470
data from both on-prem VMs
and other cloud providers.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.475
This architecture has
one main advantage.

00:01:39.475 --> 00:01:41.100
There are no additional
licensing costs

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.683
for using BindPlane.

00:01:42.683 --> 00:01:44.850
BindPlane metrics are
imported into cloud monitoring

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.460
as custom metrics, which are
charged at standard rates.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.220
Likewise, BindPlane
logs are charged

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:50.512
at the same rate as other logs.

00:01:50.512 --> 00:01:52.095
If you're looking
to monitor workloads

00:01:52.095 --> 00:01:54.000
on Kubernetes clusters
running both in Google

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.030
Cloud and elsewhere, you can use
the combination of Prometheus,

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.970
cloud monitoring, and the cloud
monitoring Prometheus sidecar

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.190
to ingest Prometheus
metrics into monitoring

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.620
for a unified
monitoring approach.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.328
Finally, you can use
a similar approach

00:02:06.328 --> 00:02:08.370
to collect logs from
multiple Kubernetes clusters

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.730
by using Fluentd, a popular
open-source logging agent,

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.100
and having it send both
infrastructure and application

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.780
logs to Cloud Logging.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.310
We've now covered
using Google Cloud

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.810
as your single pane of glass.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.310
Let's look at our
other pattern--

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.580
exporting data from Google Cloud
to our third-party service.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.770
In this scenario, there
are two primary methods

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.150
of sending that information.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.220
The first method involves
the third-party tool

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.452
integrating with a monitoring
API to consume metric data.

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:34.410
For logs, you'll have to
set up a Cloud Logging

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:35.715
export to a Pub/Sub topic.

00:02:35.715 --> 00:02:37.590
You'll then be able to
connect your preferred

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.240
tool as a subscriber
to receive those logs.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.910
For the other method,
you may choose

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.590
to leave the data
where it is and simply

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.600
aggregate at the
presentation layer.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.520
Grafana is an excellent
way to achieve this

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.145
and can be used with
multiple backends,

00:02:49.145 --> 00:02:50.898
including Cloud Monitoring.

00:02:50.898 --> 00:02:53.190
I hope this walkthrough of
common architecture patterns

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.110
to build out a hybrid
or multi-cloud approach

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.330
to observability with
Google Cloud helps you

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:58.755
as you plan your journey.

00:02:58.755 --> 00:02:59.880
That's it for this episode.

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.270
Come back soon for more.

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:02.400
This is the "Stack Doctor."

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.690
Stay healthy out there.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:07.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

